WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.560
In November 2021, the Supreme Court will hear
a case going to the core of how the United States

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:16.640
understands the human person and justice: Ramirez&nbsp;
v. Collier. the case involves Texas denying a

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:21.360
death row convict the right to audible prayer&nbsp;
and touch in prayer in his final moments.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.720
But the principles go much deeper. For centuries,
the Western legal tradition has justified capital

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:32.160
punishment partially because imminent execution&nbsp;
can facilitate repentance, thereby necessitating

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.600
the presence of ministers. And more broadly, many
religious traditions emphasize the vital role of

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:43.280
ministers giving comfort to the dying. History&nbsp;
is replete with instances of deepened faith on

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.080
death row. Protecting the presence of ministers&nbsp;
at one's final moments represents a principle

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:53.520
at the core of the American founding: Government&nbsp;
power should be shaped around religious duties.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.920
Ramirez presents the Supreme Court with an&nbsp;
excellent opportunity to continue re-anchoring

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:03.600
religion clause jurisprudence in American history&nbsp;
and tradition. Here, that means ensuring respect

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.119
for the dignity and faith of inmates in their&nbsp;last hour.

00:01:08.119 --> 00:01:13.840
What do you think? Do you think all death row inmates should have the right to&nbsp;the comfort of clergy in their final moments?

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.480
Let us know in our poll. Also let us know&nbsp;
what other topics you'd like our scholars

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.920
to cover in 60 seconds and be sure to like and&nbsp;
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

